+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+The task-handoff MCP stdio adapter (`plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py`, run as `--mcp-stdio`) rejects any `tools/call` whose `params` object carries a field other than `name` and `arguments`. Native MCP clients routinely send the standard `_meta` object inside request params (for example `{"progressToken": ...}`), so every tool call from them fails with `-32602 invalid request` before dispatch, even though the MCP protocol (version 2025-06-18, which the server already advertises) defines `_meta` as a reserved, optional metadata field. The adapter is therefore unusable from spec-compliant clients.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- The stdio server accepts `tools/call` when `params._meta` is present and is a JSON object, matching the MCP protocol's reserved `_meta` field; the value is carried nowhere — dispatch uses only `name` and `arguments`, exactly as today.
+- Every other rejection keeps working unchanged: any other unknown field in `params` (including a mistyped `_meta` that is not an object) is still rejected before dispatch; the identity-override refusal (e.g. `arguments.host`) and the oversized-frame refusal (`MAX_MCP_MESSAGE`) are untouched.
+- The Codex companion copy `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py` ships the identical file and must remain byte-identical to `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py` after the change.
+- A new contract test in `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py` proves a native-client-style `tools/call` with `params._meta` is dispatched and answered, and that non-object or other-unknown-field params are still refused.
+- Assumption (recorded per planning rules): accepting `_meta` does not bump the advertised `MCP_PROTOCOL_VERSION` (it is already 2025-06-18 and `_meta` is part of that version), and it does not bump the plugin `serverInfo` version by itself; a plugin version bump is left to normal release practice and is out of scope here.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+- `task-handoff-mcp`: behavior of the task-handoff MCP stdio endpoint — the closed JSON-RPC surface (initialize, ping, tools/list, tools/call), the strict acceptance rules for `tools/call` params (including the standard `_meta` metadata field), identity handling, frame-size bounds, and the byte-identical twin-file packaging contract between the GraphHelm plugin and the Codex companion plugin.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+<!-- No existing capability specs exist in this project yet; nothing to modify. -->
+
+## Impact
+
+- Code: `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/task_handoff.py` (`_mcp_stdio`, `tools/call` branch only); mirrored byte-for-byte into `plugins/graphhelm-codex-hooks/hooks/task_handoff.py`.
+- Tests: `plugins/graphhelm/hooks/test_task_handoff.py` (new `_meta` coverage; existing identity-override, oversized-frame, and closed-tools tests must keep passing unchanged).
+- No API/protocol surface changes beyond relaxing one rejection; no dependencies added; no other files in either plugin touched.
+- Wire-compatible: clients that never send `_meta` see exactly today's behavior.
